% A' SECTION, bars 71-82 (3/4).
% c310 (comments only; current state of v310): 71-78 the theme in Vln I and Vln II (Vln I an octave above Vln II at 71-74 b2 and from 77, at pitch
%        with Vln II 74 b3-76, c299/c309); the cellos play the counter-melody of bar 23, joined by Bsn 1 and horn 2 at 75 and horn 1 at 78 b3.
%        The cadence at 78 is deceptive to flat VI (F flat major, bass F flat2-E flat2), not to F minor: flat VI in place of natural VI as the goal
%        of a deceptive cadence (A&S Unit 23, PDF 390-391, Ex. 23-6, Madamina), which opens the short minor-coloured passage 78-81
%        (flat VI, ii6/5 at 79, ii-half-diminished 7th with F flat at 80 b3, V flat 9 at 81) that ends in the major tonic at 82.
% c023 (proposed, superseded at 78 b3 by c070): 78 b1-2 is flat VI (F flat major) under the melody's A flat, the common tone (A&S PDF 585-587, Widmung;
%        PDF 592-593, flat VI by deceptive cadence); c023's F flat-F bass on beat 3 is no longer in the score (see c070).
% c070 (proposed): 78 b3 bass F flat-E flat (passing 6/3 chord E flat-G-C between flat VI and ii6/5 at 79; c202: not a 6/4, A&S PDF 66-67), not F flat-F: flat 6 goes to 5 before natural 6 returns
%        (A&S Unit 23, PDF 388 and 395 point 3: flat6-natural6-5 is poor; cyclic-notes, mvt I c170: F flat should resolve to E flat, not to F first).
% 79-82: the cadential bars again, ff, peak B flat6 at 79 (Vln I, Fl 1; since c299 B flat6 only at 79), full close at 82.
% v03's closing 83-90 (clarinet theme head, horn motto tail over a tonic pedal) is cut (critic 003).

% c248: 78 b3 crescendo (<) in violins, cellos, basses, flute 1 and bassoons (violas left at f: their figure falls at b3 and a hairpin pushes the viola part to two pages) from the f of 78 to the ff of 79, with the horns' mf< and the harp's f stroke on the same beat (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 129-130: the anacrusis glides up into the stressed note; crescendo chords grow into the climax; Ex. 255, PDF 419: p< to sf on the last chord in every wind part)
aTwoVlnOne = {
  % c309: 77 back in the upper octave with Fl 1 (G6, a pitch the flute already sounds there), so the second octave is added with the crescendo and the winds' entry (Fl 1 + Vln I / Cl 1 + Vln II, c079) and the flute is not alone above the violins (Adler part 2 PDF 151, Mozart 40/iv; PDF 172, Meistersinger: upper octaves gradually double the violins as the crescendo builds); 75-76 stay at pitch (c299), so B flat6 is still first heard at 79
  % c299: Vln I joins Vln II at pitch from the pickup at 74 b3 to 77 (was an octave higher, F6-B flat6-A flat6 at 75-76 and G6 at 77), and reopens the upper octave on the A flat5 of the deceptive cadence at 78, so the registral peak B flat6 is first heard at 79 (Adler part 1 PDF 238: Schubert keeps the upper register back for the climactic cadence; the flute enters almost undetected at 77 and is heard only on the top notes; PDF 154: the stunning chord after a subdued, nonbrilliant register). Answers c073 (register as a goal marker) with the Adler reading; 71-74 octaves (c101) unchanged
  % c228: 76 the crescendo peaks on the B flat appoggiatura (b2; Vln I plays it as B flat5 since c299, Fl 1 keeps B flat6) and eases to its resolution (b3), then rises again from 77 b1 with the entering winds (Fl 1, Cl 1 mp<) to f at 78; mf/mp at 76 are hidden, for score.midi only. As at 12 and 28 (and c198 at 58), the dissonance carries the stress inside the build; the build's endpoints (mp 75, f 78, ff 79) are unchanged (Casanelles PDF 130, Fig. 4.4: a crescendo is shaped, not linear; PDF 127: each line its own dynamic shape, so the cello/horn counter-melody keeps rising)
  % c195: beams wholly beyond the first ledger line at 77, 78 b3, 81 (Fl 1, Vln I) slope 1/2 space in the direction of the line, not horizontal (Gould PDF 35);
  % Vln II's run at 78 b3 damped (was 1.36 spaces, eighth + close triplet semiquavers: Gould PDF 34, at most 1/2 space when notes are closer than three spaces)
  aes''4. c'''8) es'''4~(-\tweak stencil ##f \mp \< | es'''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp \> d'''4) des'''4 | c'''4.(-\tweak stencil ##f \p bes''8 aes''4 | g''2) es'8(\mp f'8 |  % c438: mp on the pickup that starts the phrase, as at 22 b3 (Sebesky PDF 205 Ex. 269, PDF 18 Ex. 2A: the new level at the phrase's first note)
 
  % c341: 77 b1 G5 at pitch (was G6): the A flat5 at 76 b3 is the 7th of ii7 over B flat1 and now resolves by step in Vln I's own line; the upper octave starts at 77 b2 on E flat6 (a rising minor sixth inside the V7 arpeggio). c309 had Vln I leap a major 7th A flat5-G6 out of the dissonance (A&S Unit 2, PDF 39 sec. 9, Ex. 2-8: dissonance approached and left by step; PDF 38: all 7ths dissonant); Fl 1 alone on G6 for one beat only, the octave pairing Fl 1 + Vln I from b2 (c079, c309)
  aes'4.\< c''8) f''4~ | f''4\upbow bes''4(-\tweak stencil ##f \mf \> aes''4)-\tweak stencil ##f \mp | g''4(\< \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.18 . -0.18) es'''8 des'''8 c'''8 bes''8) | aes''2\f \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.18 . 0.18) c'''8(\upbow\< \tuplet 3/2 { f'''16 g''' aes''') } |  % c246: the run is a second up-bow (hooked after the A flat5), so the ff B flat6 at 79 starts a fresh down-bow at the frog, as at 60-61 (Adler part 1 PDF 38, Ex. II-19/II-20: two up-bows so the accented downbeat is a down-bow; PDF 39-40: do not overload the bow in slow passages); the c118 down-bow ran about 5 s from 78 b3 through 79 at f-ff  % c118: 77 one bow, 78 A flat up-bow into the f, C6 + grace run + B flat6 one down-bow (Adler part 1 PDF 38-39); was 77-78 b2 in one down-bow (about 6 s, mf<f)  % c258: bow change on the tied F at 76 (Adler part 1 PDF 38, Ex. II-20): the B flat6 appoggiatura (c228's stress) is a down-bow, 77's crescendo an up-bow, the f at 78 a down-bow and the run a natural up-bow, so 79 still starts ff on a fresh down-bow (c246) with no hooked bow
  bes'''2.~\ff\downbow-\tag #'score ^\markup \italic "molto sostenuto" -\tag #'part _\markup \italic "molto sosten." | bes'''4(\upbow aes'''2) | g'''4(\> \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.18 . -0.18) es'''8 des'''8 c'''8 bes''8) | \after 2 \pp aes''2.\p\> |  % c028: p > pp by beat 3 so the harp harmonics (pickup to the coda) are heard (Adler part 2 PDF 503, 505)
}
aTwoVlnTwo = {
  aes'4. c''8) es''4~(-\tweak stencil ##f \mp \< | es''4-\tweak stencil ##f \mp \> d''4) des''4 | c''4.(-\tweak stencil ##f \p bes'8 aes'4 | g'2) es'8(\mp f'8 |  % c438: as Vln I
   % c338: as 23-25 (slur E flat-D, hidden MIDI levels)
  aes'4.\<^\markup \italic "ord." c''8) f''4~ | f''4\upbow bes''4(-\tweak stencil ##f \mf \> aes''4)-\tweak stencil ##f \mp | g''4(\< es''8 des''8 c''8 bes'8) | aes'2\f \once \override Beam.damping = #5 c''8(\upbow\< \tuplet 3/2 { f''16 g'' aes'') } |  % c118: as Vln I  % c258: bow change on the tied F at 76 (Adler part 1 PDF 38, Ex. II-20): the B flat6 appoggiatura (c228's stress) is a down-bow, 77's crescendo an up-bow, the f at 78 a down-bow and the run a natural up-bow, so 79 still starts ff on a fresh down-bow (c246) with no hooked bow
  bes''2.~\ff\downbow^\markup \italic "molto sostenuto" | bes''4(\upbow aes''2) | g''4(\> es''8 des''8 c''8 bes'8) | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t aes'2\p\> r4\! |  % c063: releases on b3 with the violas, so the harp's harmonic pickup E flat4-F4 is not inside a sustained A flat4/E flat4 (Adler part 2 PDF 274, 267)
}
aTwoFluteOne = {
  R2.*4 |
  R2.*2 | g'''4(\mp\< \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.18 . -0.18) es'''8 des'''8 c'''8 bes''8 | aes''2)\f \breathe \temporary \override Stem.direction = #DOWN \temporary \override TupletBracket.direction = #DOWN \once \override Beam.positions = #'(-0.18 . 0.18) c'''8(\< \tuplet 3/2 { f'''16 g''' aes''' } \revert Stem.direction \revert TupletBracket.direction |  % c385: c195's ledger-line slope (-0.18 . 0.18) restored, lost when c375 replaced the line, and the tuplet bracket on the beam side as in Vln I/II (Gould PDF 35: ledger-line beams take a slight slope, 1/2 space for wider intervals, and matching angles; PDF 36: slope in the direction of the outside interval)  % c375: 78 b3 down-stems, as Vln I/II at 78 b3 and Fl 1 at 77 and 81: every note of the group lies above the stave (Gould PDF 27: notes above the centre line take down-stems; PDF 28: stems of ledger-line notes reach the middle line, so the beam sits inside the stave, clear of the ledger lines, PDF 33). The partcombiner's voice one gave up-stems because flute 2 enters at 79; \temporary so the revert restores the combiner's direction for 79.  % c275: the 78 b3 Beam.positions override removed: it was set for the down-stems that c275 found came from bSec's \stemNeutral; with the up-stems c105 wanted the fixed positions gave negative stems  % c105: grace stems up as in the violins; down-stems put the beam among the ledger lines (Gould PDF 33)  % c178: mp (was p, c123): G6 opens the flute register Adler marks "a bit shrill" (part 1 PDF 185, Ex. VII-2); matches clarinets 1-2 mp< in the same bar, still under the violins (Rimsky PDF 127)
   % c041: breath between the slurs before the ff B flat6 (Adler part 1 PDF 188). c036: B flat6 approached by scale, as in the violins (Adler part 1 PDF 186)
  bes'''2.~\ff | bes'''4 aes'''2) \breathe | g'''4(\> \temporary \override Stem.direction = #DOWN \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.18 . -0.18) es'''8 des'''8 c'''8 bes''8 \revert Stem.direction | \after 2 \pp aes''2.)\p\> |  % c394: 81 b2-3 down-stems kept explicitly: with flute 2 now sounding on 81 b1 the part-combiner gives flute 1 up-stems, which the beam positions above do not fit (as at 78 b3; Gould PDF 27-28)
}
% c014: flute 2 and oboe 2 double the upper harmony against four horns ff (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 108-109).
% c394: flute 2 keeps its octave over oboe 2 into the F flat (80 b3-81 b1), as oboe 2 (c234): the inner F was doubled in Fl 2 + Ob 2 at 79-80 b2 and lost its upper octave
% exactly where it turns into F flat, the note of the passage to be made prominent; the Ab5-Ab6 octave was left empty at ff (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 90 rule 1: doubled or not throughout,
% except when a part is to be made prominent; PDF 96 rem. 1: no void in the middle of close wind harmony, worst in forte). Re-struck after the shared breath, released with oboe 2.
aTwoFluteTwo = { R2.*8 | f'''2.~\ff | f'''2 fes'''4 \breathe | fes'''4-\tag #'part \> r2-\tag #'part \! | \tag #'score s2. \tag #'part R2. | }  % c395: score spacer at 82, as oboe 2 (c265): flute 2's whole-bar rest hung below the stave with no ledger line, under flute 1's dotted half and through its p>pp hairpin (Gould PDF 49, 51)  % c394: hairpin in the part only; on the shared staff flute 1's hairpin runs on to the p at 82 (a score hairpin ended by this rest stopped the shared one at 81 b2)
aTwoOboeTwo = { R2.*8 | f''2.~\ff | f''2 fes''4 \breathe | fes''4-\tag #'part \> r2-\tag #'part \! |  % c394: hairpin in the part only, as flute 2: on the shared staff oboe 1's diminuendo now runs to the p at 82 instead of stopping at 81 b2
  \tag #'score s2. \tag #'part R2. | }  % c265: score spacer at 82, as c115/c155 for the flutes: oboe 2's whole-bar rest hung below the stave with no ledger line and oboe 1's rest on b3 was pushed up a space for it; one centred shared rest instead (Gould PDF 49: rests stay centred; PDF 51: parts share a rest wherever possible, minim and semibreve rests outside the stave sit on a ledger line)  % c234: F flat5 at 80 b3 and 81 b1 (was a rest), re-struck after the breath oboe 1 already takes at the barline (no tie through the shared comma): the woodwind choir had no F flat, the note that makes 80 b3 half-diminished and 81 b1 the minor ninth, while Hn 3 and the lower violas carried it alone at F flat4 (Sebesky PDF 102: voice each body of instruments as a complete-sounding entity; PDF 110 Ex. 136: an incomplete woodwind voicing sounds harsh; PDF 81: a third from each neighbour, A flat5 above, D flat5 below). Released on 81 b2, where oboe 1 sings the resolution E flat5; c054 kept (no G4-A flat4 doubling in the diminuendo).  % c054: oboe 2 leaves before the diminuendo; its G4-A flat4 doubled Cl 2, Vla and (at 82) oboe 1 in unison (Adler part 1 PDF 249-250, 270)  % c024: F5 fills the sixth between D flat5 and B flat5 (Rimsky-Korsakov appendix PDF 505-507: close spacing in the upper winds; PDF 125); B flat4 at 80 b3 leads to the old G4
% c187: the cor anglais joins the violas on the theme's lowest octave at the ff peak, so each of the three octaves is a string group with a wind
% (Fl 1 + Vln I / Ob 1 + Cl 1 + Vln II / Vla + C.a.): Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 76 (a melody over three octaves is hard to balance; his examples give
% every octave a combined group), PDF 76-77 (Violas + Eng. horn among the best unisons; Sadko Ex. 82-83), PDF 78 (Sheherazade iii [M]:
% Vns I + Fl / Vns II + Ob / Cellos + Eng. horn). It follows the violas note for note, leaves the theme with them at 81 b2 (c074) and releases with them at 82 b3 (c063).
aTwoCorAnglais = {
  R2.*8 |
  bes'2.~(\ff | bes'4 aes'2) \breathe | g'4(\> es'2 | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t es'2)\p\> r4\! |
}
aTwoOboeOne = {
  R2.*8 |
  bes''2.~(\ff | bes''4 aes''2) \breathe | g''4(\> es''8 des''8 c''8 bes'8 | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t aes'2)\p\> \tweak staff-position #0 r4\! |  % c265: centred (oboe 2 has no rest here in the score; as flute 1 at 63)  % c063: as Vln II
}
aTwoVla = {
  c'8(\pp^\markup \italic "ord." es' aes') es'( c' aes) | c'( aes') bes( aes') bes( g') | c'( es' aes') c'( es' aes) | des'( es' g') es'( bes des') |  % c127: 74 b1 D flat4 (was B flat3 on the cellos' B flat3)  % c060: D flat4 (7th of V4/3) resolves to C4 at 75 (was bes( es' g') es'( bes es'))  % c085: "unis." dropped here and at 79: no divisi since 64 (Adler part 1 PDF 30: unis. cancels div.)
  c'(\p\< es' aes) es'( aes f') | f'( aes' f') bes( f' bes) | g( es' g') es'( bes g) | \tag #'part { \once \override Score.MetronomeMark.X-offset = #-4 \once \override Score.MetronomeMark.extra-offset = #'(0 . -0.8) } aes(\f fes' aes') fes'( c' g) |  % c285: part only: "poco allargando" (78) printed under bar 65 of the system above, clear of its own system, because it stood over the up-bow at 80; moved left over the clef (MOLA Ex. 3, PDF 10) and down, it sits on its own system (MOLA PDF 8, Ex. 7)  % c127: 76 b3.5, 77 b1.5 and b3.5: the figure no longer strikes the counter-melody's D flat4 and B flat3 (Vc, Hn 2) on its own notes (Adler part 2 PDF 259-260, 274); 78 b3 C4 kept (E flat4 there moves in octaves with the bass's F flat-E flat)  % c324: 75 b2 A flat3 (was A flat4): since c299 both violin sections hold the theme's A flat4 here, so the figure's top note struck the only sounding octave of the melody in the same string colour (Adler part 1 PDF 135; c177's pulsation reading, PDF 262, is a wind doubling of the tune in a second colour)
  % c042: 80 b3 F flat4 (with horn 3): F-F flat-E flat (6-flat 6-5) into 81, ii-half-diminished 7th before V7 (A&S PDF 391-392, 395)
  bes'2.~\ff-\tag #'score ^\markup \italic "molto sostenuto" -\tag #'part _\markup \italic "molto sosten." | bes'4(\upbow <f' aes'~>4-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \italic "div." -\tag #'part _\markup \small \italic "div." <fes'~ aes'>4) | <fes' g'>4(\> es'2-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \italic "unis." -\tag #'part _\markup \small \italic "unis.") | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t es'2\p\> r4\! |  % c227: lower half F4-F flat4-E flat4 with horn 3 (restores c042's 6-flat6-5 line in the strings; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 113: horns doubled by divided violas); upper half keeps c071's third octave with the cor anglais  % c074: the third octave leaves the theme at 81 b2 (was es'8 des'8 c'8 bes8 | aes2): its D flat4-C4-B flat3-A flat3 ran into and under the cello/horn counter-melody (D flat4-C4), unison at 81 b2.5 and crossed at 82 (Adler part 1 PDF 144: theme and countertheme separated registrally; PDF 142: order of priority)  % c071: the theme's third octave at the peak (Adler part 2 PDF 24, Ex. XI-8: Elgar's octave and double-octave doublings; PDF 23: the melody notes to stress made to dominate); F and A flat stay in Fl 2, Ob 2, Hn 3-4, Cl 2, harp  % c063: the violas' E flat4 was the harmonic pickup's own pitch (sounding E flat4) on b3; they release with Vln II and Ob 1
}
aTwoVc = {
  es2\p^\markup \italic "cantabile, sul D" aes8( g8 | f2) g4( | aes2\< c'4)\> | bes4( aes4) g4\! |  % c386: sul D 71-77 as at 23; "ord." at 78 returns the A string for the f-ff climb C flat4-C4-D flat4 into 79 (Adler part 1 PDF 99: the A string the most brilliant)  % c302: 74 b2 passing A flat3 (was bes2), as c301 at 26: a passing 7th over the V4/3's bass B flat1 (A&S PDF 406, Ex. 24-9: a 7th may arise as a passing tone in a descending third), on the weak beat between consonant B flat3 and G3; no parallels with Vla/Vln (checked)  % c118: g4 its own bow, so the crescendo at 75 is an up-bow and the f at 78 a down-bow (Adler part 1 PDF 38)  % c032: as at 26 (fifths with the bass into 75; A&S PDF 266)
 
  es2(\mp\< f4) | aes2( des'4) | bes2( des'4) | ces'2\f^\markup \italic "ord." c'4\< |
  des'4\ff^\markup \italic "molto sostenuto" c'4 bes4 | aes2 bes4--~ | bes4\> des'2-- |  % c217: 79 ff with the string tutti (was f from 78, c074): in a mixed tutti the strings are the weakest element (Sebesky PDF 168), and the same line is ff in bassoon 1 (shared staff with Bsn 2); a wind is marked below the strings it doubles, not above (Sebesky PDF 164). Horns 1-2 stay f (c034/c174)  % c118: one bow per hemiola half note (A flat | B flat | D flat), so the diminuendo at 82 is a down-bow (Adler part 1 PDF 38)
  \after 2 \pp c'2.\p\> |  % c048: hemiola 80-81 (A flat 2 | B flat 2 | D flat 2): two bars of 3/4 heard as one bar of 3/2 before the cadence (A&S PDF 51, Ex. 3-8)  % c074: 79 stays f from 78 (was ff), as horns 1-2 (c034): the counter-melody on the cello's A string is its most intense register and is middleground under the ff theme (Adler part 1 PDF 135; PDF 144: dynamics graded)
}
aTwoHnOne = {
  R2.*7 | \partCombineUnisono r2 c'4(\mf\< |
  des'4\f-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "a2" c'4 bes4) \breathe | aes2( bes4--~ | bes4\> des'2-- | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t c'2)\p\> r4\! \partCombineAutomatic |  % c048: hemiola 80-81 with the cellos; breath moved to the end of 79  % c034: f, not ff (also horn 2): the counter-melody stays middleground under the theme (Adler part 2 PDF 147, 154)
}
% c006: the 71-78 counter-melody moves to horn 2, so horn 1 rests after its solo at 67-70.
% c067: horn 2 joins the cellos' counter-melody at 75 with the crescendo, not at 71: horn and cello in unison is an intense sound to be used sparingly
% (Sebesky PDF 166), and here it already returns at 79-82 (Hn 1-2, Vc, Bsn 1); 71-74 keep the cellos alone, as at 23-26. Was es2\p aes8( g8 | f2 g4 | aes2 c'4 | bes2 g4) |
aTwoHnTwo = {
  R2.*4 |
  es2(\p\< f4 | aes2 des'4 | bes2 des'4)\mp | r2 c'4(\mf\< |  % c217: p< to mp (was mp< to mf): one horn on the cellos' mp line is marked a level below them (Sebesky PDF 164); a horn on a cello line adds intensity and is used sparingly (PDF 166), so its full weight is kept for 78 b3-82 (Hn 1-2 a2)  % c035: the crescendo ends on mf at 77 b3, not on the p of the new entry
  des'4\f-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "a2" c'4 bes4) \breathe | aes2( bes4--~ | bes4\> des'2-- | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t c'2)\p\> r4\! |  % c048: hemiola 80-81
}
% c037: at 80 the inner parts (Hn 3-4, Vla, Cl 2, Ob 2) hold instead of repeating quarters, so the only quarter-note motion
% is the counter-melody (Vc, Hn 1-2, Bsn 1) against the held B flat6-A flat6 (Adler part 2 PDF 230: lean texture makes the big chord tell;
% PDF 217: two layers in the same note values need separating).
aTwoHnThree = { R2.*8 | f'2.\f | f'2-\tweak stencil ##f \f fes'4~(-\tweak stencil ##f \ff \< | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t fes'4-\tweak stencil ##f \ff \> es'2) | R2.\!^\markup \small \italic "mettere sord." | }  % c082: F flat4 held over the bar as a 9-8 suspension: V minor-9th by mixture (A&S PDF 464 Ex. 27-3; PDF 465 Ex. 27-4c: the 9th as a common tone from II; PDF 466 Ex. 27-5: 9-8 inside V), resolving to E flat4 as the 7th D flat enters  % c064: advance mute warning, five bars before con sord. at 87 (Adler part 2 PDF 50, Ex. XI-27/XI-29: mutes marked where they go on and off)  % c024: horns 3-4 leave first in the diminuendo (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 130). c014: horns 3-4 in the middle of the chord, bass left to Bsn 2 and B.cl. (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 101, 108)
aTwoHnFour = { R2.*8 | aes2.\f | aes2~-\tweak stencil ##f \f aes4(\< | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t g2-\tweak stencil ##f \ff \> es4) | R2.\!^\markup \small \italic "mettere sord." | }  % c122: 81 b3 E flat3, not G3 held: the leading tone was doubled (Cl 2 G4, Hn 4 G3) on the beat before the goal tonic and horn 4's G3 stopped unresolved; G3 now moves to the root inside V7 and only Cl 2's G4 carries the leading tone, which resolves (A&S PDF 267, 286: doubled leading tone not before a goal tonic)  % c044: horns 3-4 f, not ff, at 79: sustained harmony inside the horn counter-melody's register is not marked louder than the counter-melody (Hn 1-2 f) (Adler part 2 PDF 184: dynamics graded by importance; PDF 159 rule 1)
aTwoClOne = {
  % c067: rests 71-74 and joins the lower octave at 75 with the crescendo, as flute 1 joins the upper octave: at 71-74 the lower octave (Vln II + Cl 1)
  % outweighed the lead (Vln I alone) (Sebesky PDF 138 rule 2: the lead line carried by more players than any other line; PDF 164: one woodwind weighs
  % about as much as sixteen violins; PDF 139 rule 4: the higher line needs more players). Was aes'4.(\pp dolce c''8 es''4~ | es''4 d''4 des''4 | c''4. bes'8 aes'4 | g'2) r4
  R2.*4 |  % c059: clarinet 1 doubles the theme's lower octave with Vln II from 71 (Brahms 1/ii reprise re-scored; Adler part 1 PDF 238, 255, 267 Ex. VIII-38)  % c036: breaths at 74 b3, 78 b3 and after 80 (Adler part 1 PDF 175, 188)
  % c079: rests 75-76 and joins at 77 with flute 1 (c078), so the two octaves gain a wind each at the same moment: with flute 1 gone from 75-76
  % the lower octave (Vln II + Cl 1) again outweighed the lead (Vln I alone), the imbalance c067 removed at 71-74 (Adler part 2 PDF 209: the melodic
  % figure emphasised by the most doubling; PDF 213, Bizet 13-18: the crescendo orchestrated by adding instruments). Was aes'4.(\p\< dolce c''8 f''4~ | f''4 bes''4 aes''4 |
  \partCombineSoloII R2.*2 | \partCombineAutomatic g''4(\mp\< es''8 des''8 c''8 bes'8 | aes'2)\f r4 |
  bes''2.~(\ff | bes''4 aes''2) \breathe | g''4(\> es''8 des''8 c''8 bes'8) | \after 2 \pp c''2.\p\> |  % c034: theme's middle octave with Ob 1 and Vln II, so the B flat5 line outweighs the ff counter-melody (Adler part 2 PDF 151: Mozart 40/iv, the heaviest-scored octave is heard as the theme)
}
aTwoClTwo = {
  % c247: 71 and 75 b1 E flat4 (was A flat4): the clarinet no longer holds the theme's first note A flat4 in unison with Vln II under the attack (Adler part 1 PDF 135: background kept off the foreground's pitch; PDF 128 rule 3)
  es'2.(\pp | aes'4.-- bes'8~ \tag #'score \once \stemUp bes'4 | aes'2 es'4 | des'2) r4 |  % c324: 73 b3 E flat4 (was A flat4 held through the bar): Vln II arrived on clarinet 2's sounding A flat4 at 73 b3, the fault c247 removed at 71 and 75; A flat4 kept on b1-2 (c247's E flat at b1 made fifths with the bass), E flat4 is the seventh of vi7 (c060) and falls to D flat4 at 74 (Adler part 1 PDF 135: background kept off the melody's pitch when colours are close; PDF 128 rule 3)  % c126: legato slurs with soft-tongued repeats (Adler part 1 PDF 176-177); breaths unchanged  % c442: 72 b2 A flat4 held (was F4 eighth): the 3rd of vi7 stays as the 7th of V7/V on the beat, as Bsn 1 at 8 and the violas at 24 (A&S PDF 400, Ex. 24-3: interlocking sevenths, each chord's 3rd prepares the next 7th as a common tone); it leaves for the B flat4 anticipation (c061) and resolves into the violas' G4 at 72 b3.5, the next lower voice in the same register (PDF 406-407, Ex. 24-11)
   % c073: 74 D flat4 (was G4, a unison with Vln II): the 7th of V4/3 sustained, so the chord over the B flat bass is complete (A&S PDF 127 sec. 7); it passes to the violas' D flat4-C4 (74 b3.5-75), the last voice that has it (PDF 102 sec. 15)  % c059: B flat4 at 72 b3: G4-A flat4 moved in octaves with the horn 2/cello counter-melody's G3-A flat3 into 73  % c036: breaths at 74 b3, 77 b3 (staggered with clarinet 1) and after 80  % c061: B flat4 anticipated on 72 b2.5 (A&S PDF 363-366): F4-B flat4 over B flat1-E flat2 made parallel 5ths with the bass at 72 b2-b3
  es'2(\p\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \bold "2." des'4 | aes'4-- f'2) | g'2\mp\< r4 | \tag #'score { r2\f } \tag #'part { r2 } g'4-\tag #'part \f -\tag #'score -\tweak stencil ##f \f |  % c145: in the part the f stood under the half rest; it now marks clarinet 2's own entry on b3 (Adler part 2 PDF 301, Ex. XVIII-10b: each part carries its own dynamics); the score is unchanged (shared f on b1, "2." at b3)  % c252: 75 b3 D flat4 (was E flat4 held): over the IV at 75 b3 the held E flat4 was an unresolved ninth that left by leap to A flat4; D flat4 doubles the root (A&S PDF 76, 82)
    % c144: rests at 78 b1-2 (was aes'2, a unison with clarinet 1 on the theme's A flat4 at f): two like winds in unison only thicken the pitch outside a full tutti, and the A flat stays in octaves with flute 1 (Adler part 1 PDF 249-250, 257); the f on the rest keeps one shared dynamic  % c124: mp (was mf) so clarinet 1's entry on the lower octave is not louder than flute 1's p< on the upper octave (c123); keeps c079's balance of the two octaves (Adler part 2 PDF 150-151)  % c079: mf< with clarinet 1's entry (shared staff, one dynamic)
  f'4\ff des''2 | des''2.~ | des''4\> \tag #'part \breathe g'2 | \after 2 \pp aes'2\p\> \tag #'score \once \override Rest.staff-position = #-4 r4 |  % c292: D flat5 tied into 81 b1 (was breath after 80, G4 from 81 b1): the V flat 9 on the hemiola's second unit now has its 7th D flat with the F flat, i.e. the complete vii°7 G-B flat-D flat-F flat over the E flat root (A&S PDF 378-379 Ex. 22-9; PDF 393 Ex. 23-9, Chopin in A flat), the 7th held as a common tone from the ii half-diminished 7th at 80 b3 (PDF 377-378, Ex. 22-7b); D flat resolves to C in the melody at 81 b3 (PDF 380 Ex. 22-5, transferred resolution); breath moved to after 81 b1, printed in the part only (on the shared staff a mid-bar comma would read as clarinet 1 breaking its slurred eighths)  % c123: F4 on 79 b1 (was A flat4): the resolution A flat no longer sounds a second under the violas' B flat4 appoggiatura (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 132-133); Hn 4 keeps A flat3  % c063: releases on b3 (A flat4 above the harmonic pickup); clarinet 1's pp ends the shared hairpin  % c034: takes clarinet 1's D flat5 (keeps c024's fill between D flat5 and B flat5); G4 at 81, not B flat4, so no D flat-B flat octaves with the cellos, horns and Bsn 1; A flat4 held over the bar line at 79 so it does not leap A flat-D flat in octaves with the cellos
}
% c094: bassoon 1 joins the cello/horn 2 unison from 75 (p<, as bassoon 2 on the shared staff): horn and cellos alone in unison 'do not blend so well', each heard too distinctly; 'the combination of the three different timbres in unison produces a rich, mellow and coherent tone', the bassoon linking wood-wind and brass (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 51-53).
% c011: bassoon 1 joins the cello and horn counter-melody at the ff return (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 54, 56: relief by doubling; Antar [63] cellos doubled by bassoons).
aTwoBsnOne = { aes2)\pp r4 | R2.*3 | es2(\p-\tag #'part \< f4 | aes2-\tag #'midi \mp des'4 | bes2 des'4-\tag #'part \mf -\tag #'midi \mf) \breathe | ces'2-\tag #'part \f c'4(\< | des'4\ff c'4 bes4) \breathe | aes2( bes4--~ | bes4\> des'2-- | \after 2 \pp c'2.)\p\> | }  % c048: hemiola 80-81  % c134: 78 b1-2 C flat4 with the cellos (was r2): the F flat chord of the deceptive cadence had no C flat in the winds and the counter-melody lost its wind colour for two beats (Adler part 2 PDF 162, 164, 176)  % c400: MIDI-only ('midi tag, kept only in score.ly's MIDI \score) mp (76) and mf (77 b3) so score.midi follows the part's p< mf f; on the shared staff Bsn 1 had only its p and played every note of 75-78 at velocity 32 while Bsn 2, Hn 2 and the cellos it doubles crescendoed (c338/c340 open item; A&S PDF 348; Sebesky PDF 241: compare the score with the recorded result). At 78 the two bassoons share one voice on the staff and Bsn 2's printed f already sets Bsn 1's level. Print unchanged.
% c176: breath at the end of 77 (the slur ends there; horn 2 rests 78 b1-2 but bassoon 1 plays on): 75-79 b3 was about 17.7 s without a breath, p< to ff (Sebesky PDF 24 item 11, PDF 30 Ex. 33)
aTwoBsnTwo = {
  c2.(\pp | f4 bes,4 es4 | aes,2 f4 | bes,2) r4 |  % c126: slurs (Adler part 1 PDF 176)
   % c060: with the basses (was aes,2. | es2 r4)  % c052: 71 C3 resolves the D flat3 of 69-70 in its own line; 73 A flat held (no A flat-C octaves with the cello/horn counter-melody)  % c036: breaths at 74 b3, 77 b3 and after 80; the double basses keep the bass
  aes,4(\p\< c4 des4 | des2-- bes,4 | es2) r4 | fes2(\f es4\<) |
  des2.\ff | des2 bes,4 \breathe | es2.\> | \after 2 \pp aes,2.\p\> |
}
% c024: contrabassoon doubles the double basses at the A' peak, so the ff bass has more than the double basses at 16' pitch
% (Rimsky-Korsakov, appendix of single tutti chords, PDF 505-507: every ff chord doubles the bass in the low octave with C-fag./tuba + Cb.).
aTwoCbsn = { R2.*8 | des,,2.\ff | des,,2 bes,,,4 \breathe | es,,2.\> | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t aes,,2\p\> r4\! | }  % c184: 79-81 an octave lower (D flat1-B flat0-E flat1, was in unison with the basses' D flat2-B flat1-E flat2), as at 61 (c133): the bass doubled in the octave below (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 82, 85; Adler part 1 PDF 230: the lowest octave is its most effective range, a 32-foot foundation doubling the basses at the octave); the second ff peak now has the first peak's bass depth  % c176: breath at the end of 80 with bass clarinet and bassoon 2 on the same line (79-82 b2 was about 14 s at ff without one; Sebesky PDF 70, 74, PDF 30 Ex. 33)
aTwoBassCl = { R2.*8 | des2.\ff | des2 bes,4 \breathe | es,2.\> | \after 2 \pp aes,2.\p\> | }
aTwoCb = {
  % c348: "ord." cancels the basses' "sul A" of 65 (retrCb); every other string name in the movement is cancelled by "ord." or the next string name (Adler part 1 PDF 64: the string must be carefully marked, so its end must be too; as Vla "ord." at 71, Vln II at 75). Text only
  \set Staff.midiExpression = #1.0 aes,,2.\pp^\markup \italic "tutti, ord." | f,4 bes,,4 es,4 | aes,,2 f,,4 | bes,,2. |  % c060: 73-74 I-vi7 | V4/3 (was A flat held | E flat, as at 25-26): the reprise's first phrase is not harmonised as at 23-26 (Sebesky PDF 19-20, Ex. 3/3A)  % c052: as at 25
  aes,,4\p\<\downbow c,4 \once \stemUp des,4 | \once \stemUp des,2 bes,,4 | es,2. | fes,2\f es,4\< |  % c186: 75 down-bow (retaken after the half cadence at 74), so the crescendo at 77 is an up-bow and the f at 78 and ff at 79 are down-bows (Adler part 1 PDF 38: louder and heavier at the frog, crescendo natural on the up-bow; mark bow direction only where the count would go against it)
  des,2.\ff^\markup \italic "molto sostenuto, change bow at will" | des,2 bes,,4 | es,2.\> | aes,,2.~\p\> |  % c256: 79-80 at poco allargando (crotchet 48) are a 3.75 s ff down-bow and, with c186's hooked B flat1, a 3.9 s ff up-bow on the short bass bow; marked as at 61 (Adler part 1 PDF 108: 'thicker, heavier, and quite a bit shorter ... ask the players to change bow at will'); the section still attacks 79 down-bow from c186's plan, the hook at 80 b3 is dropped  % c186: 80 b3 a second up-bow (Adler part 1 PDF 38, Ex. II-19: two up-bows so the next downbeat is a down-bow; the winds breathe here), so the diminuendo at 81 is a down-bow  % c063: diminuendo to the coda's ppp (83), tied into the coda pedal, so the pedal is already sounding under the harp's pickup (Adler part 2 PDF 192)
}
aTwoTimp = { R2.*6 | R2.^\markup \small "medium sticks" | r2 es4:32\pp\< | des2.:32\f^\markup \small \italic "ord." | des2.:32 | \once \override Staff.Hairpin.circled-tip = ##t es2.:32\> | R2.\! | }  % c397: 78 b3 E flat roll p< into the f roll at 79 (Adler part 2 PDF 184: the crescendo to the climax is aided by a timpani roll marked piano and then crescendo, as at 60)  % c123: no A flat2 stroke at 82; the E flat roll is answered by the timpani's own E flat-C-A flat at 84-85 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136: a group silent for some time gains fresh interest on its reappearance)  % c056: single stroke, no hairpin (a struck note cannot diminish); damped on b3 under the harp harmonics (Adler part 2 PDF 80)
aTwoHarpR = {
  R2.*4 |  % c034: RH tacet 71-74: its chords were the viola figure's own notes in the same octave on the same beat (Adler part 2 PDF 158: no two instruments with identical parts for more than a few notes); it re-enters after a rest with the crescendo at 75 (PDF 156)
  r4 <aes es'>4\arpeggio r4 | <des' f'>4\arpeggio r2 |  % c247: 75 b2 C4 dropped: over the bass C (I6) the third is doubled only at the bottom (Cb, harp LH, Bsn 2), as c207 did for the violas at 27 (Adler part 1 PDF 152, Ex. V-27)
  r4 <g es'>2\arpeggio | r2 <g es'>4\arpeggio |  % c127: 76 without A flat3, 77 G3-E flat4 (was G3-B flat3-D flat4), 78 b3 G3-E flat4 (was G3-C4-E flat4): no attacks on the counter-melody's A flat3, B flat3, D flat4 and C4 (Adler part 2 PDF 259-260, 274)
  R2.*3 | r4 <aes c'>4\arpeggio es'8 f'8 |  % c116: pickup at pitch, not harmonics: at 82 b3 flutes, clarinets, B.cl., bassoons, Vln I, Vc and Cb still hold the p>pp chord; harmonics start at 83 over the ppp pedal only (Adler part 1 PDF 116, rule 1)  % c026: octave harmonics, written an octave below the sound (Adler part 1 PDF 116); hidden voice carries the sounding pitch to MIDI  % c019: harp head starts here (pickup), so the timpani answer and the Vln I tail in the coda do not overlap
}
aTwoHarpL = {
  \set Staff.ottavationMarkups = #ottavation-numbers \ottava #-1 % c025: as at 7-14 (Gould PDF 46-47)
  <aes,, es,>2.\pp\arpeggio-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \harp-pedal #"^-^|^--^" | <f,, f,>4\arpeggio \once \stemUp <bes,, bes,>4\arpeggio es,,4 |  % c089: 72 octaves and 73 b3 single F, not fifths: A flat-E flat/F-C/B flat-F were consonant parallel fifths (A&S PDF 341); a dyad F1-F2 at 73 b3 made fifths F/C -> E flat/B flat with the cellos into 74
  <aes,, es,>2\arpeggio f,,4 | <bes,, bes,>2.\arpeggio |  % c114: B-flat octave, no fourth in the lowest octave (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 124). c060: with the basses (was <aes,, es,>2. | <es,, es,>2.)
  <aes,, es,>4\p\<\arpeggio c,4 \once \stemUp des,4 | <des, aes,>2\arpeggio bes,,4 | <es,, es,>2.\mp\arpeggio | \tag #'part \ottava #0 r2 \tag #'part \ottava #-1 <es,, es,>4\f\arpeggio \ottava #0 |  % c365: in the part 78 starts a system; the 8 now stands at the first note (b3), not under the rest (Gould PDF 42, 44)  % c244: mp at 77 (was mf): the crescendo ends at the level of the cello/horn 2 counter-melody (mp) whose register (B flat3-D flat4) the RH G3-E flat4 encloses; f with the orchestra at 78 b3 kept (Casanelles PDF 287: sounds in one frequency range mask each other; c241 option)  % c116: harp grows with the orchestra (p<mf, f at 78 b3; Adler part 1 PDF 113-114: middle register rich, carries)  % c115: line ends after the last note before the 79-81 rest (Gould PDF 45: separate signs across extensive rests; PDF 44: corner after the notehead)  % c114: octaves as at 10 and 13 (no fifth E-flat1-B-flat1 or sixth E-flat1-C2 in the lowest octave: Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 124-125)
  R2.*3 |  % c079: no re-entry on 81 (was <es,, bes,,>2.\mf\>, RH r4 <g bes des'>2): after the 79-80 rest a fresh mf attack fell inside the orchestrated diminuendo; the harp returns p on the tonic at 82 (Adler part 2 PDF 213, 224: the diminuendo made by instruments dropping out)  % c076: tacet 79-80 under the ff tutti (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 47: three or four harps in unison to be heard against a full orchestra forte)
  \ottava #-1 << { <aes,,~ es,>2.\p\arpeggio } { s2 s4\pp } >> |  % c266: pickup E flat4-F4 at pp, the level of the harmonics it leads into and of the orchestra at the end of 82 (Adler part 1 PDF 116 rule 1)  % c214: A flat1 tied into 83 (let ring), so the harmonics enter without a low-string attack (Adler part 1 PDF 116 rule 1)  % c115: new 8 bassa sign just before the first note after the rests (Gould PDF 44-45)  % c026: 8 bassa continues into 83 (no loco needed)  % c019: harp dies away with the orchestra (was f to the end)
}
